\fs28 SPAWN COMMANDS\par
\fs20 Spawn commands will do just that, spawn things. You can spawn just about everything in the game.\par
\par
\b SPAWN_NPC\par
\b0 This command will allow you to spawn an NPC onto the map. The NPC will not be persistent, and will disappear when you warp out of the sector. You can load up an existing NPC ship file (MOD/ships_npc/) or you can create a brand new npc_ship.txt file and load that instead. The newly spawned NPC will not be aggressive, and will use it\rquote s own AI to determine its behavior. \par
\par
Value 1: NPC_ship_filename.txt ( MOD/ships_npc/ )\par
Value 2:X Y Z position ( x.x#y.y#z.z ) (Example: 100.5#250.2#23.6 )\par
Value 3 Faction ID  (MOD/factions/faction_database.txt)\par
Value 4 NPC Level ( Whole numbers only  )\par
Value 5 NPC Name ( Optional, leave empty for random name generation. )\par
\par
\i Notes: You can use this command for a variety of different scenarios, but don\rquote t over do this one too much, you can slow down the game if too many NPCs buzzing about.\par

\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf2\brdrbtw\brdrbar \widctlpar\sl276\slmult1\i0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl276\slmult1\par
\par
\b SPAWN_RAIDERS\par
\b0 This command function much like the SPAWN_NPC command, except that these are the baddies, and are always hostile. You can spawn raiders fairly easily, it is killing them that is the hard part. This command will only spawn one type of raider ship, but you can adjust the quantity of raiders to be spawned.\i\par
\i0\par
Value 1: NPC_ship_filename.txt ( MOD/ships_npc/ )\par
Value 2:X Y Z position ( x.x#y.y#z.z ) (Example: 100.5#250.2#23.6 )\par
Value 3 Number of Raiders to spawn ( Whole numbers only )\par
Value 4 NPC Level ( Whole numbers only  )\par
\par

\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf2\brdrbtw\brdrbar \widctlpar\sl276\slmult1\i Notes: If you want to spawn a variety of different raiders, chain together a few of these commands with different ship files, and quantity numbers on each command.\i0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl276\slmult1\par
\par
\b SPAWN_CRATE\par
\b0 This command will spawn a CRATE onto the sector map. The crate will contain whatever you specify in Value 1. You can place the crate wherever you like using the xyz data stored in Value_2. Great little command for all kinds of tasks and quests.\par
\par
Value 1: ItemID#qty,ItemID#qty\par
Value 2:X Y Z position ( x.x#y.y#z.z ) (Example: 100.5#250.2#23.6 )\par
Value 3 Faction ID  (MOD/factions/faction_database.txt)\par
Value 4 Crate owner\rquote s name ( String of characters  )\par
\par

\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf2\brdrbtw\brdrbar \widctlpar\sl276\slmult1\i Notes: none\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl276\slmult1\i0\par
\b SPAWN_RESOURCE\par
\b0 This command will spawn a RESOURCE onto the sector map. The size of the resource can be set with Value 3, and a special name for the resource can be set in Value 4.\par
\par
Value 1: Resource ID\par
Value 2:X Y Z position ( x.x#y.y#z.z ) (Example: 100.5#250.2#23.6 )\par
Value 3 Resource Size ( Whole numbers or Decimal )\par
Value 4 Resource special  name ( String of characters  )\par
\par
\i Notes: none\par

\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw30\brdrcf2\brdrbtw\brdrbar \widctlpar\sl276\slmult1\b\i0\par

